      "question": "Can you explain the difference between supervised and supervised learning and can you give me examples which respect the robotics?",
      "answer": "In supervised learning, a model is trained on labeled data to learn the relationship between inputs and outputs. For example, in robotics, imagine you have a dataset of images of different objects with their corresponding labels (e.g., 'chair', 'table', etc.). You train a computer vision model to recognize these objects based on their visual features. Once trained, this model can be used for tasks like object detection or classification. On the other hand, unsupervised learning involves training a model on unlabeled data to discover patterns or relationships within that data. In robotics, you might use unsupervised learning to cluster similar sensor readings from a robot's environment, without knowing what specific objects are present. This can help the robot learn to recognize and adapt to its surroundings.",
      "full_answer": "In supervised learning, a model is trained on labeled data to learn the relationship between inputs and outputs. For example, in robotics, imagine you have a dataset of images of different objects with their corresponding labels (e.g., 'chair', 'table', etc.). You train a computer vision model to recognize these objects based on their visual features. Once trained, this model can be used for tasks like object detection or classification. On the other hand, unsupervised learning involves training a model on unlabeled data to discover patterns or relationships within that data. In robotics, you might use unsupervised learning to cluster similar sensor readings from a robot's environment, without knowing what specific objects are present. This can help the robot learn to recognize and adapt to its surroundings.",
